\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Rupiah corrects after rally  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2005\par 03:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 12 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah fell more than a percent on Monday in what traders said was an overdue correction after last week's sharp gains that were driven by a surging stock market and a government reshuffle.  \par \par \uc2  Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were flat to slightly weaker, in line with the Japanese yen <JPY=>, ahead of another expected U.S. rate rise on Tuesday, trade and inflation data from the United States and Japan's quarterly tankan business sentiment survey.  \par \par \uc2  The rupiah <IDR=> fell to about 9,760/9,770 per dollar, down from Friday's 5-1/2-month peak of 9,625.  \par \par \uc2  "The rupiah recovered too strongly last week. There was bound to be some reversal," said Vikas Suri, head of global markets at Standard Chartered Bank in Jakarta.  \par \par \uc2  The rupiah gained more than 3 percent last week, which took its rise in the last 3 weeks to 5 percent, as foreigners poured money into Indonesian stocks and high-yielding bonds.  \par \par \uc2  A rate rise last Tuesday and expectations that a team of economic ministers sworn in last week would push for faster reforms and toughen the fight against Indonesia's mounting inflation boosted market sentiment.  \par \par \uc2  Suri said the rupiah would adopt a range of 9,700 to 9,800 per dollar while markets waited for the new economic team to deliver on their promised reforms.  \par \par \uc2  Markets were also cheered last week by news that Indonesian state oil firm Pertamina and U.S. giant Exxon Mobil were close to resolving their dispute on who should operate the Cepu oil project on the island of Java, which would boost the country's oil output.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the rupiah's rally last week, which nearly matched a rise in the key stock index <.JKSE>, had been too rapid.  \par \par \uc2  Mirza Baig, Deutsche Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, said he would be a cautious buyer of rupiah.  \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentally, I am inclined to be a bear, but it is hard to ignore the constructive near-term story," he said.  \par \par \uc2  "The JKSE is still doing well, and broader sentiment within Indonesia remains oddly positive despite the prospects of slower growth and higher inflation next year."  \par \par \uc2  Besides, Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  much and would welcome some rupiah strength to help the economy contain inflation, Baig said.  \par \par \uc2  CHINA SPECULATION  \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Philippine peso <PHP=>, Asia's best performer this year and one of last week's outperformers, shed more than a quarter of a percent from Friday's 2-\u189\'00\'BD   year high of 53.38 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2  China's markets shrugged of a rush of comments from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Zhou Xiaochuan on Monday dismissed as nonsense speculation of a major shift in policy towards the yuan at the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2  One-month non-deliverable forwards <CNYNDFOR=> priced the yuan at around 8.0490 per dollar, unchanged from Friday and factoring in a 0.35 percent appreciation from the spot rate of 8.0773.  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   Change on the day at 0817 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       120.81         120.65       -0.13\par  Sing dlr        1.6827         1.6837       +0.06\par  Taiwan dlr      33