\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Bank Indonesia backs rupiah markets, as last resort  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gde Anugrah Arka  \par \uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2005\par 07:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds ANALYSIS tag to headline)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Oct 18 (Reuters) - Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made itself available as the counterparty of last resort for investors seeking protection against a choppy rupiah, a move analysts say will make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market more liquid and more stable.  \par \par \uc2 But the terms that Bank Indonesia offers have so far tended to be more expensive than market rates, ensuring that banks will turn to it only during periods of high volatility.  \par \par \uc2 The rupiah <IDR=> was languishing near its weakest levels in four years in August when Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BI) decided that, in addition to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market, it would conduct one- to seven-day \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps to regulate rupiah supply and demand.  \par \par \uc2 It followed that up on Oct. 3 with a hedging facility for investors to swap dollars into rupiah and back for three- to six-month periods.  \par \par \uc2 Similar practices had been in place before the Asian financial crisis of 1997/98, of which the rupiah was a big casualty.  \par \par \uc2 They are back now. As investors rushed for cover against a tumbling rupiah, BI felt the need to re-use swaps as a policy tool.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts lauded the move. By \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through swaps -- a derivative that combines a spot and forward exchange of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will add volume to the forward rupiah market. At times, it can suck out excess rupiah funds that could be used for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation.  \par \par \uc2 Its offer to be a counterparty to banks, with clients wanting to hedge rupiah assets, will bring investment flows that the country badly needs, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "For sure they will deter speculation and they will be providing liquidity," said a Jakarta-based bank treasurer.  \par \par \uc2 "But the pricing and conditions are such that this swap window will remain a facility that people will use in periods of extreme volatility."  \par \par \uc2 MARKET RUCTION  \par \par \uc2 In a typical transaction where a foreigner makes a fully hedged, six-month investment in Indonesia, the foreigner would sell dollars for rupiah to invest in rupiah assets. The investor would also buy six-month forward dollars for rupiah.  \par \par \uc2 If both transactions were to be done simultaneously, the investor could simply enter into a swap with a bank, which involves selling dollars for rupiah immediately in the spot leg and buying dollars for rupiah in the forward leg.  \par \par \uc2 After the events of recent months, the reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's swap move is clear.  \par \par \uc2 Heavy imports of oil and investor demand for higher Indonesian yields drove the rupiah down to a four-year low of 11,750 per dollar on August 30, a 21 percent loss in 8 months. It has since recovered 16 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Demand for forward dollars surged in July and August and banks found it difficult to square their swap positions in the market. Local banks had more difficulty finding foreign counterparties because their credit ratings are mostly below investment grade.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  compounded the problem, analysts said. Seeking to defend the rupiah, in July it limited the access offshore players had to the forward rupiah market, making swap transactions more scarce.  \par \par \uc2 In early September, the premium on six-month dollars <IDRF=> shot up to an annualised 14 percent, from 6 percent at the end of July. The premium is now 10 percent, partly owing to higher official interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Catherinawati Hadiman, corporate banking director at Indonesia's seventh-largest lender PT Bank Niaga, said the swap offers could attract more foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows.  \par \par \uc2 "Hopefully this could help stabilise the rupiah."  \par \par \uc2 LAST OPTION  \par \par \uc2 Sukatmo Padmosukarso, a director at the country's sixth largest lender, PT Bank Internasional Indonesia (BII), said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's measure should help market sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 "Particularly for us here at BII, 30 percent of our third party (depositors) funds were denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We need such schemes to help our customers," Padmosukarso said. Businesses could even borrow dollars and swap it into rupiah instead of taking a loan at home, although it is more expensive. Three-month rupiah costs about 13 percent <JIBO11>. Against that, on Monday it cost 4.2 percent to borrow dollars plus 11 percent to swap the dollars into rupiah, totalling 15.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 If a bank swapped 3-month dollars for rupiah with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the cost would have been higher at 16 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said there were days when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pricing was more expensive, and markets would turn to it only as the last option.  \par \par \uc2 Still, analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's guiding hand in markets could go a long way in helping the government find overseas finance for some $145 billion of infrastructure projects planned over the next 5 years.  \par \par 