\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Min: Parliament Talks Mon On Fuel Subsidy Cuts  \b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2005\par 11:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's parliament will start discussions Monday on details of the government's planned fuel price subsidy cuts, Energy and Mineral Resources Minister Purnomo Yusgiantoro said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "The government will consult with the Parliament on Sept. 5 on the size of fuel prices increases," Purnomo told reporters, without elaborating.  \par \par \uc2 President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono said Wednesday that the government plans to take the politically unpopular move of slashing fuel price subsidies and raising fuel prices "after October", but didn't mention the size or specific timing of the cuts. [ 01-09-05 0933GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Yudhoyono said the government must finalize plans for direct cash subsidies to Indonesia's lower-income earners prior to a further fuel price increase. Yudhoyono's government already slashed fuel subsidies and hiked fuel prices by an average 29% on March 1.  \par \par \uc2 The government is scrambling to accelerate preparations for subsidy cuts after negative market and analyst reaction to the rupiah rescue measures announced Wednesday which failed to deliver specific details about the cuts.  \par \par \uc2 Yudhoyono said Wednesday that fuel price subsidy costs could reach IDR138.6 trillion in 2005. That projection outpaces the $7.4 billion, or 3% of gross domestic product, that fuel price subsidies drained from state coffers in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Fuel price subsidy costs have soared as Indonesia, the sole Southeast Asian member of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries, increased oil imports due to dwindling domestic oil output.  \par \par \uc2 Its imports are rising in tandem with a surge in crude oil prices on the global market.  \par \par \uc2 Supply worries have sharply boosted global crude oil prices in recent weeks. On Tuesday, crude futures for October delivery settled at a record $69.81 a barrel on the New York Mercantile Exchange as Hurricane Katrina ravaged the U.S. Gulf Coast, disrupting production at key oil and gas facilities. At 1056 GMT Thursday, the benchmark front-month contract was up 31 cents at $69.25/bbl.  \par \par \uc2 Prior to Yudhoyono's speech Wednesday, the dollar closed at IDR10,200 from its previous close of IDR10,525, on positive sentiment about the government's apparent determination to stop the rupiah's fall.  \par \par \uc2 Thursday, the dollar crept up to an intraday high of IDR10,802 on what analysts say is market disappointment over Yudhoyono's apparent lack of resolve on the fuel price subsidy cut issue. The dollar later eased to close at IDR10,225.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Coordinating Minister for the Economy Aburizal Bakrie denied comments he made earlier in the day about the possibility of advancing fuel subsidy cuts to September instead of "after October" to help give the rupiah traction against the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 "Your (reporter)) friends were sleeping," Bakrie said in response to requests that he clarify his earlier statement.  \par \par \uc2 "What I want is for ministers to work quickly to finish (subsidy cut) preparations in September."  \par \par \uc2 Those preparations include a joint census by the Central Statistics Agency and National Planning Agency of lower-income Indonesians eligible for income support to cushion the impact of a fuel price subsidy cut.  \par \par \uc2 The government hopes direct cash payments to that segment of Indonesia's population will thwart possible public protests when fuel subsidies are eventually cut.  \par \par \uc2 Bakrie said census data for Indonesia's main island of Java will be ready on Sept. 15, and for the remainder of Indonesia on Sept. 30.  \par \par \uc2 "Only after that can we have fuel price hikes," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Bakrie also downplayed the effectiveness of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan to require Indonesian-based firms to repatriate export revenue to bolster \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in support of the rupiah.  \par \par \uc2 Those reserves have been bled by more than $5 billion since April by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed to boost the rupiah.  \par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah said Monday that the "administrative burden" of enforcing export revenue repatriation made the policy impossible.  \par \par \uc2 "The forced repatriation will only manage to bring...$10 billion in one year and 80% of this belongs to foreign investors, so the impact isn't going to be significant," Bakrie said.  \par \par \uc2 "The most important thing is to reduce subsidies which is going to have more significant impact on fiscal policy."  \par \par \uc2 -By Jakarta Bureau; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; dj.jakarta@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Genevieve Soledad [ 01-09-05 1113GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050901e191000ig\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
