\par\par \b \uc2 Japan MOF:BOJ, Bk Indonesia Signed 2nd  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Swap Agreement  \b0\par\par\uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2005\par 05:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan and Bank Indonesia signed a second bilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap agreement Wednesday, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.  \par \par \uc2 The agreement will allow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps of up to $6 billion between monetary authorities in the two countries. [ 31-08-05 0313GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The latest agreement comes as Indonesian policy-makers announced a raft of measures to stem a recent and rapid decline in the rupiah due to record high oil prices and costly government subsidies on fuel prices.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia on Tuesday raised the target for benchmark one-month interest rates by 75 basis points to 9.5% and tightened the money supply by requiring banks to hold more money at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The rupiah has been steadily declining for much of this year and hit a four-year low of IDR11,900 to the dollar Tuesday before rebounding in response to Bank Indonesia's rate hike. At 0357 GMT, the rupiah was quoted at IDR10,410.  \par \par \uc2 State-owned oil company Pertamina (PTM.YY) needs as much as $1.8 billion a month to finance its oil imports, and that has gradually weighed on the rupiah, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian government also devotes about a quarter of all government spending to fuel subsidies, which has rattled confidence in the rupiah as these subsidies seem unsustainable, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia has spent more than $5 billion of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves since April in repeated attempts to defend the rupiah by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono projected that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves may fall to $27.1 billion by the end of next year from $32 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Japan and Indonesia's second \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap arrangement doubles the amount of transfers under a previous agreement, which is in line with a pact by finance ministers from Japan, China, South Korea and the Association of Southeast Asian nations to significantly expand their existing $39.5 billion network of bilateral swaps in May.  \par \par \uc2 The second swap agreement between Japanese and Indonesian monetary authorities falls under the Chiang Mai Initiative, a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps set up following the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis to help cash-strapped countries defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in times of crisis.  \par \par \uc2 -By Stanley White, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; stanley.white@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 31-08-05 0411GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050831e18v00051\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
