\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Indonesia Sells Dollars To Defend Rupiah: Dealers  \b0\par\par\uc2 214 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2005\par 09:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank Indonesia sold dollars Wednesday to try to slow a continuous slide in the rupiah, dealers told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar came off its intraday high of IDR10,320 because Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that level," a dealer at a domestic commercial bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The IDR10,320 level marks the lowest point the rupiah has hit against the U.S. dollar since Feb. 7, 2002. The dollar hit an intraday high of IDR10,335 on that day.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers are currently quoting the dollar at IDR10,280-IDR10,300.  \par \par \uc2 Investors' "panic buying" of U.S. dollars prompted by the steady decline in the rupiah combined with corporate dollar purchases for overseas obligations are resisting Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the local unit, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers predict that the dollar may breach the IDR10,500 resistance in the near term unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the government of President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono make politically painful policy choices.  \par \par \uc2 Those policies include loosening inward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, raising the benchmark Bank Indonesia rate and slashing budget-crippling fuel price subsidies, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 By Jakarta Bureau; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; dj.jakarta@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Costas Paris [ 24-08-05 0802GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050824e18o000at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
