\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Stocks lend support, but rupiah weakens  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2005\par 06:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 22 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on Monday, supported by rallying regional stock markets, a higher Japanese yen and a slight drop in global oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> was the exception to the trend. It hit a fresh 3-1/2-year trough and then recovered slightly after Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars, repeating a pattern seen last week.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> was emerging Asia's biggest gainer of the day. It rallied nearly 0.7 percent to near 41 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> fell to 56 per dollar and recovered to end the day a quarter of a percent higher. The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> and the Korean won <KRW=> <KRW=KFTC> also gained about a quarter of a percent each.  \par \par \uc2 "The main driver today has been stronger equity markets in a few selected countries and the weak rupiah," said Ray Farris, markets strategist with Credit Suisse.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei share average <.N225> ended at a four-year high on Monday, causing other regional stock markets <0#.INDEXA> to rally, brushing aside worries over dearer oil.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices <CLc1> were slightly softer than the record highs over $67 a barrel hit this month, with U.S. September light crude futures trading just over $65.  \par \par \uc2 Farris said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had priced in the current levels of oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 "If oil rises substantially from here, then that will be distinctly bearish for a lot of Asia, particularly the countries that are already running weaker current account positions, for instance Thailand, India, Taiwan," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Lehman Brothers said in a note the market was, quite needlessly, reluctant to buy the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fearing the impact of oil prices, deteriorating trade balances and the yen being overbought.  \par \par \uc2 "Net-net, the market is once again trying to talk itself out of buying Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a cycle we have gotten used to in 2005," Lehman economists said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 "With Japanese markets finally waking up, regional growth still sound and inflation risks beginning to build, we are not going to follow suit - stay long yen, Singapore dollar and Taiwan dollar," they wrote.  \par \par \uc2 RUPIAH DIVERGES  \par \par \uc2 The rupiah hit 10,025 per dollar in morning deals, its weakest since March 2002, deepening its losses in the past 10 days to more than 2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted selling dollars to stem the rupiah's decline. Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor Aslim Tadjuddin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary to restrain rupiah volatility.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also offered a 3-day deposit to mop up funds and scrapped its repurchase facility on Bank Indonesia bonds, both moves aimed at tightening short-term rates and making it more expensive to speculate against the rupiah.  \par \par \uc2 The rupiah is Asia's worst performer so far this year, with rising oil imports and foreign debt repayments weighing heavily on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Indonesian stock market <.JKSE> has lost 9 percent in the past 10 sessions.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia said in early July that Pertamina would get dollars to pay for its oil imports from the government's account with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the oil firm has been spotted buying dollars in the market.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said Pertamina had probably been forced to go to the market again for its dollar needs, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  got worried about how fast its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were being depleted.  \par \par \uc2 An Indonesian business newspaper reported on Monday that Pertamina planned to raise at least $500 million through bonds next year to finance crude oil imports and strengthen its working capital.  \par \par \uc2 Anton Gunawan, an economist with Citigroup, said the fund raising would help the oil firm overcome its immediate cash shortfall, while alleviating pressure on the rupiah. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0838 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       109.68         110.74       +0.97\par  Sing dlr        1.6675         1.6685       +0.06\par  Taiwan dlr      32.110         32.186       +0.24\par  Korean won     1022.60        1025.50       +0.28\par  Baht             41.00          41.30       +0.73\par  Peso             55.86          56.05       +0.33\par  Rupiah         9999.00        9985.00       -0.14\par  Rupee            43.60          43.59       -0.02\par  Ringgit         3.7680         3.7650       -0.08\par  Yuan            8.1057         8.1047       -0.01\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       109.68         102.45       -6.59\par  Sing dlr        1.6675         1.6310       -2.19\par  Taiwan dlr      32.110         31.920       -0.59\par  Korean won     1022.60        1035.10       +1.22\par  Baht         