\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Rupiah Ends Down Despite Ctrl Bank Moves   \b0\par\par\uc2 876 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2005\par 14:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--The Indonesian rupiah closed below the key level of IDR10,000 to the U.S. dollar late Friday, despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealers expect the dollar to continue rising Monday as companies with dollar exposures get jittery and jump on the dollar-buying bandwagon.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at IDR10,000, versus the previous close of IDR9,950, after going as high as IDR10,010 during the day. The close was the highest since March 8, 2002, when it ended at IDR10,015.   \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia aggressively sold dollars for the third straight day in a failed attempt to keep the unit below the threshold of IDR10,000. State banks also sold dollars, keeping the unit below IDR10,000 for a time, but market players eventually pushed it over the top after Bank Indonesia ended its daily trading activity.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually finishes dealing at 0900 GMT, but interbank players sometimes continue trading until there are no further quotations in the market.   \par \par \uc2 "Players continued buying dollars until the last minute since they expect more foreign investors to exchange their rupiah into dollars Monday," said a dealer with a foreign bank.   \par \par \uc2 Market participants were also expecting increased dollar demand Monday after the local stock market Friday fell 1.1%, the sixth consecutive day of losses.   \par \par \uc2 The Jakarta Stock Exchange's Composite Index fell 12.346 points to 1087.953 Friday, up from its intraday low of 1081.724 on expectations the falling rupiah might prompt an increase in the benchmark Bank Indonesia rate.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased the rate to 8.75% last week from 8.50% in a bid to curb inflation and support the rupiah.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, Economics Minister Aburizal Bakrie said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only be "temporarily" effective, and that pressure on the rupiah would continue if Indonesia doesn't reduce its fuel imports.   \par \par \uc2 Later, Bank Indonesia Deputy Governor Aslim Tajuddin blamed the rupiah's fall on "external factors," including soaring global oil prices and the upward trend in U.S. interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 "Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  (to support the rupiah) if it is necessary and will continue to monitor fluctuations in the (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market," Aslim said. He also said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had managed to reduce volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 Aslim said Indonesia's macroeconomic fundamentals remain sound; but he said that for a stronger rupiah, the country needs more exports, an improved investment climate and reduced imports of consumer goods and fuel.   \par \par \uc2 An Asia-based syndicate banker with a large U.S. house said that if the rupiah stays weak or weakens further, investors will likely lose their appetite for Indonesian U.S.-dollar corporate bonds - both in the primary and secondary markets - as the issuers' burden of interest payments grows.   \par \par \uc2 At the same time, rupiah weakness could trigger hedge-fund buying in rupiah-denominated debt, as investors position themselves for a rupiah rebound, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said they expect the dollar to continue rising Monday, as companies begin to grow panicky and rush to buy dollars. Dealers said Bank Indonesia will likely try to calm the market by starting the week with aggressive dollar sales to bring it back below IDR10,000.   \par \par \uc2 "The first thing Bank Indonesia has to do Monday is push the greenback below IDR10,000 to give the impression that its rise to IDR10,000 was only a blip," said a dealer with a local bank. "Otherwise there will be panic dollar buying."   \par \par \uc2 But such efforts will likely to fail, said Citigroup economist Anton Gunawan.   \par \par \uc2 "They will try very hard to defend it, but the pressure is so high from dollar-buying by (state-owned oil firm) Pertamina and other corporates that we can still see (the exchange rate) go beyond IDR10,000," Gunawan said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar could linger above IDR10,000 for a month, meeting strong resistance at the IDR10,200 mark, Gunawan said.   \par \par \uc2 To provide long-term support for the rupiah, Gunawan said, the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  need to raise the benchmark Bank Indonesia rate and cut fuel price subsidies, he said.   \par \par \uc2 ABN Amro Bank's regional head of sovereign and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy warned that Bank Indonesia's dollar-selling to support the rupiah won't work, and is bleeding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to potentially dangerous levels.   \par \par \uc2 "If (Bank Indonesia) wants to just defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the end they'll lose," Irene Cheung said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should instead let the dollar rise to an "appropriate value," she said, without offering a specific projection.   \par \par \uc2 "If they allow the rupiah to adjust to a more appropriate level, it will mean less pain for the economy ... otherwise we're looking at foreign reserves sinking to $27.1 billion by the end of next year, which is dangerous for Indonesia's ratings."   \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's has plowed more than $5 billion into defending the rupiah since April. The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves currently stand at $32 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the dollar to trade between IDR9,950 and IDR10,025 Monday.   \par \par \uc2 -By I Made Sentana and Phelim Kyne, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; I-Made.Sentanadowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 19-08-05 1202GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1159 1848   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050819e18j000l3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
