\par\par \b \uc2 Asia Benchmark Bonds Weaker, Oil Worries Take Shape   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Oliver Biggadike   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 794 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2005\par 11:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Yield spreads on Asian U.S. dollar bonds widened slightly this week, with Indonesian credits by far the worst performers as investors turned bearish on the country's growth prospects in the face of rising oil prices.   \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, analysts said that spreads could widen further if economic data show any signs that oil prices, which remain above $60 a barrel, are hurting economic activity and, more specifically, company profits.   \par \par \uc2 While oil companies clearly benefit from high oil prices, others for whom oil is a cost - such as airlines and power companies - could suffer.   \par \par \uc2 "September will be quite a critical month," said Dilip Shahani, head of fixed-income research at HSBC. "Once there's a suggestion of a global slowdown, there will be knock-on effects on assumptions for corporate earnings."   \par \par \uc2 Though analysts say a slowdown is unlikely to create outright defaults, declining company profitability would lead investors to demand a higher risk premium to hold corporate credit, pushing out spreads between yields on corporate debt and U.S. Treasurys.   \par \par \uc2 The widening in the broader market this week was modest. JPMorgan's Asia Credit Index composite rose only three basis points to 140 basis points.   \par \par \uc2 But the Indonesian component of the index widened 23 basis points in the week ending Thursday to a 290-basis-point spread over U.S. Treasurys. Over the same time period, the price of five-year Indonesian credit default swaps, which protect against an issuer's inability to pay, widened to 255-280 basis points from a bid price of 230 basis points.   \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the market, the usually heavily traded benchmark bonds from Hutchison Whampoa Ltd. (0013.HK) were quoted at 119-115 basis points over U.S. Treasurys midday Friday, wider than the 113-110 basis point spread quoted a week earlier.   \par \par \uc2 Bucking the trend were PCCW Ltd.'s (PCW) 2011 bonds for which the spread tightened toward the end of the week and quoted a basis point tighter Friday at 116-106 basis points from Thursday. The move followed PCCW's first-half earnings report, which showed a declining pace of market-share loss in its fixed-line division.   \par \par \uc2 "There's nothing to be really happy about (in the PCCW earnings), but it's not bad either," said Vijay Chander, senior credit analyst at BNP Paribas in Hong Kong. "PCCW still has some way to go before they become a growth company and can offer returns to shareholders."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    High Oil Prices Trouble Indonesia \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns about Indonesia stem mostly from the impact of rising oil prices, which are particularly harmful for developing economies as fuel isn't used as efficiently as in industrialized economies and they lack extensive buffers to cushion the shock.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Indonesia's case, the increased cost of importing oil has put downward pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , forcing Bank Indonesia to spend \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to stem the rupiah's fall. So far it hasn't had much success and many expect the dollar will push above the key psychological level of IDR10,000. In such an environment, investors are naturally nervous.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Friday, the U.S. dollar was trading at IDR9,985 after Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the rupiah, selling more of its dwindling supply of U.S. dollars between IDR9,985 and IDR9,990, according to traders.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In Indonesia, (oil is) a problem," said HSBC's Shahani. The rise in oil prices is likely neutral in the short term, but it raises the risk that government tax revenues fall as growth slows, he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is in a difficult position. If it hikes rates to maintain price stability and support the rupiah, it wins credibility at the expense of slower growth in the short term. If Bank Indonesia does nothing, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely continue to fall, lifting inflation and sapping growth.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "At the moment, people are sitting on the fence," Shahani said. If Bank Indonesia does hike rates enough to get inflation under control, the local debt market could see a bull flattening as the market prices out some of the risk of holding longer-dated IDR-denominated bonds, he added.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most analysts expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's main interest rate to reach 9.75% by year end from 8.75% now.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the other hand, a persistently weaker rupiah would likely prompt some portfolio shift out of local debt and into Indonesian U.S. dollar bonds.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Paradoxically, people may buy (Indonesian) U.S. dollar bonds if the rupiah weakens," said BNP's Chander.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Oliver Biggadike, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4044; oliver.biggadike@dowjones.com.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 19-08-05 0904GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 4262 5097   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050819e18j000ez\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
