\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Indonesia Fuel Subsidies Blowing Out Budget   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Phelim Kyne   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2005\par 07:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia must raise fuel prices by early 2006 in order to ease the pressure of fuel price subsidies on the cash-strapped government, Indonesia's Vice President Jusuf Kalla said recently.   \par \par \uc2 Record high global oil prices above $60 per barrel have created an unbearable subsidies burden for the government, Kalla told Dow Jones Newswires.   \par \par \uc2 "I think all of us understand that the price of oil is (around) $62 per barrel, which means our subsidies are too high right now," Kalla said.   \par \par \uc2 "We can't afford them and that's why we should increase (fuel) prices."   \par \par \uc2 The government will increase fuel prices "no later than early next year," he said, without elaborating. That suggests a retreat from statements Kalla made Wednesday indicating a fuel price increase before the end of 2005.   \par \par \uc2 Kalla's comments reflect how record-high global crude oil prices are forcing the government of President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono to consider the politically risky move of raising fuel prices less than a year after a highly unpopular average 29% fuel price increase on March 1.   \par \par \uc2 Fuel subsidies cost the government around $7.4 billion in 2004, or 3% of gross domestic product. Indonesia's Minister of Finance Jusuf Anwar said last month that fuel subsidies may rise to IDR123 trillion this year, well above the IDR78 trillion estimated in the official 2005 budget.   \par \par \uc2 Government assurances that it would keep price subsidies for kerosene, the fuel of Indonesia's poor, dissipated scattered public protests that greeted the March fuel price increase.   \par \par \uc2 But a second price hike may fan popular discontent with Yudhoyono's administration into large-scale and potentially destabilizing public protests that plagued the administration of his predecessor, Megawati Sukarnoputri.   \par \par \uc2 The size of the pending fuel price increase depends on factors such as fluctuations in the price of crude oil and the value of the rupiah against the U.S. dollar, Kalla said.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Cash Subsidies For Poor To Deter Protests \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government also blunted criticism of the March fuel price increase by promising to direct the bulk of proceeds from the hike to fund social programs for the estimated 16.5% of Indonesia's people who live under the official poverty line. Indonesia's poverty line stands at around IDR130,000 per month for city dwellers and around IDR100,000 per month for rural residents.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kalla said that the government hopes to dampen the potential for such demonstrations of popular discontent with the pending fuel price increases through direct cash subsidies to the country's poor, prior to the increase.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Of course there (will be) protests," he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "(But) local governments, our statistics office...are doing a census of poor people and then we'll directly subsidize the people and after that we'll increase the price of (fuel)," Kalla said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government is also facing a cabinet shuffle before the end of 2005, Kalla said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The performance of individual ministers in their first year in office will be carefully evaluated and any possible cabinet post changes will likely occur in October, he said, without elaborating.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kalla said a pending peace deal between separatist insurgents and the Indonesian government in tsunami-devastated northwestern Aceh province will likely boost the exploration \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of U.S. petroleum giant ExxonMobil Corp. (XOM), which has oil and liquid natural gas \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the troubled province.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Representatives of the government and the Free Aceh Movement, or GAM, will sign an official truce on Aug. 15 in Helsinki, Finland.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Exxon of course can explore more (after the peace agreement) and if Exxon explores more, there are taxes and money for the government," Kalla said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kalla indicated that he was uncertain whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move Tuesday to raise the benchmark Bank Indonesia rate to 8.75% from 8.50% would achieve one of its desired effects of supporting the rupiah.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Inadequate Funds For Avian Flu Control \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupiah's health hinged on factors other than interest rates, including the relative strength of the U.S. dollar and Indonesia's export performance, Kalla said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupiah has lost around 5% of its value against the U.S. dollar since the beginning of the year and Bank Indonesia has sacrificed $5 billion, or 14% of total \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kalla said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government is unable to reallocate budgetary resources to bolster the Ministry of Agriculture's losing battle to combat H5N1 avian flu outbreaks among domestic poultry stocks.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Minister of Agriculture Anton Apriyantono said last month his ministry can't undertake international-standard culling of poultry in H5N1 outbreak areas due to a lack of government funds to compensate farmers for their losses.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 International concerns about Indonesia as a potential weak link in the global effort to prevent or contain a possible future outbreak of a more deadly human variant of H5N1 have risen since the country reported its first three human fatalities from the illness last month.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Avian flu has killed 61 people in Asia and devastated poultry stocks in the region since late 2003.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We haven't enough budget (funds) if we slaughter (poultry) according to a five-kilometer (affected) area parameter," Kalla said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead the government supports "selective" poultry culling in areas where H5N1 outbreaks occur, he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Phelim Kyne; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; phelim.kyne@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mui Khi Lim [ 11-08-05 0507GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050811e18b00055\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
