\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Indonesia Rate Hike Targets Inflation, Oil Price  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Phelim Kyne  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2005\par 14:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank Indonesia, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Tuesday it had hiked its benchmark Bank Indonesia rate to 8.75% from 8.50% to address inflationary pressure driven by rising oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 "Inflation expectations remain high in the wake of rising oil prices, which could increase (domestic) prices and weaken the rupiah," a Bank Indonesia statement issued late Tuesday said. "The increase in the Bank Indonesia rate is considered to be capable of supporting the continuity of the economic recovery."  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices have hovered around a record high $60 per barrel in recent months. The September Nymex crude oil price stands at $63.76 per barrel, down 18 cents versus Monday's floor close.  \par \par \uc2 Surging oil prices have accelerated inflationary pressure in Indonesia, Southeast Asia's sole member of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries, as dwindling domestic output has forced the government to steadily increase the proportion of imported oil in its national energy mix.  \par \par \uc2 But state-owned oil firm Pertamina's (PTM.YY) voracious appetite for dollars to fund imports has contributed to a 5% erosion in the value of the rupiah against the greenback since the beginning of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Coordinating Minister of the Economy Aburizal Bakrie told reporters Tuesday that declining domestic output will make Indonesia a net crude oil importer for 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the rate increase was overdue and linked to the toll taken on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem a steady slide in the rupiah since the beginning of the year. The rupiah closed at IDR9,795 Tuesday, down from IDR9,772 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the rupiah and couldn't continue to do that," Standard Chartered Bank economist Fauzi Ichsan told Dow Jones Newswires. "The alternative method to support the rupiah is raise short-term interest rates."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts Say Rate Hike Overdue  \par \par \uc2 The increase in the Bank Indonesia rate comes after criticism from analysts who have argued that the bank has hesitated to raise rates due to political rather than macroeconomic concerns.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has avoided aggressive interest rate increases to address inflation for fear of undermining the government's official gross domestic product growth target of 6.0% in 2005. That projection outpaces last year's 5.4% economic expansion.  \par \par \uc2 The rupiah has borne the brunt of that monetary policy choice, they say.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 5% of its value since the start of the year, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  running down its foreign reserves by $5 billion to defend the unit.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently abandoned an original average IDR9,300 target for 2005 and Bakrie has predicted the unit will average IDR9,750 in the second half of 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The bank statement said the rate increase also aims to offset the "increased risk to macroeconomic stability" of other outside influences, including rising U.S. Federal Reserve rates and a weakening of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy rate has risen 225 basis points to 3.25% since June 2004, while the dollar has strengthened markedly against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. Market participants anticipate that a U.S. Federal Reserve policy meeting later Tuesday may lead to a 25-basis-point increase in the Federal Funds target rate to 3.5%.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia's emphasis on the potential domestic economic impact of the rising Fed rate is a welcome proactive move, said Standard Chartered's Ichsan.  \par \par \uc2 "Many analysts are upwardly revising forecasts of U.S. interest rates to 4% by the end of the year...and think the Fed rate will rise to 4.5% next year," he said.  \par \par \uc2 This will also prompt Bank Indonesia to "make a move especially when it has been criticized for being behind the curve in raising interest rates," he said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Phelim Kyne; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; phelim.kyne@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Farida Husna of Dow Jones Newswires Jakarta bureau contributed to this story)  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 09-08-05 1207GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050809e189000r0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
