\par\par \b \uc2 Bk Indonesia: Can't Rely On Rate Hike To Cut Inflation  \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2005\par 11:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't rely on a possible interest-rate rise to stem rising inflation, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 He also said that Indonesia's economic growth rate remains below its potential and said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to support the rupiah.  \par \par \uc2 "What we are facing now is the dilemma of boosting macro-economic growth and maintaining macro-economic stability," Perry Warjiyo, head of the economic research and monetary policy directorate at Bank Indonesia, said Friday. "Relying on increases in interest rates will not be enough."  \par \par \uc2 On the rupiah, Warjiyo said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  "regularly" to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But he also noted that the rupiah had lost 3% of its value against the U.S. dollar in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The official didn't give any further detail on the timing of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his comments at an economics forum.  \par \par \uc2 At the start of August, the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned that the rupiah could weaken further as the Indonesian balance of payments pushes into deficit for the first time since 2001.  \par \par \uc2 The rupiah is down around 4% versus the U.S. dollar from the start of the year, but has gained gradually in recent weeks after reaching its weakest point this year on July 5.  \par \par \uc2 UBS estimates that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  since April cost the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost $5 billion, or 14% of its total reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Increases in government-controlled prices have pushed up inflation, and the International Monetary Fund has said Bank Indonesia may need to raise its benchmark one-month rate, currently at 8.5%.  \par \par \uc2 -By Oliver Biggadike, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4044; oliver.biggadike@dowjones.com.  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 05-08-05 0926GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Warjiyo said the best way to tackle rising prices is for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to attack core inflation by increasing interest rates, while the government takes administrative measures to head off noncore inflation, which includes volatile food and energy prices.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coordinating with the government in this area, Warjiyo said; for example, the government has relaxed tariffs to ease import prices.  \par \par \uc2 Warjiyo estimates that Indonesia's core inflation in the second quarter was 6.8%, while headline inflation was 7.42%.  \par \par \uc2 -By Oliver Biggadike and Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4044; oliver.biggadike@dowjones.com or saeed.azhar@dowjones.com [ 05-08-05 1027GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050805e185000dv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
