\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Indonesia's inflation targeting hostage to rupiah  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 817 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2005\par 06:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 7 (Reuters) - Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has embarked on a policy of transparent interest-rate and inflation targeting, a laudable effort in the eyes of financial markets but one that economists say will prove challenging.  \par \par \uc2 The jury is still out on whether Bank Indonesia's new policy tools will lead to more stable growth and prices, given that it has to juggle the management of a volatile rupiah with calls from Indonesia's coalition government for lower interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 For the first time this month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has announced a target for the yield on one-month certificates, aimed at hitting an explicit target for inflation.  \par \par \uc2 That was a marked shift from its policy of the last six years of steering yields through market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and trying to hold down inflation through managing base money -- a policy widely seen as opaque and hard to convey to the public.  \par \par \uc2 But success hinges on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , since the rupiah's value influences the price of everything from the huge amounts of oil Indonesia imports to the commodities it exports. And the rupiah's fortunes are linked to the government's economic reforms and the ability to attract foreign investment.  \par \par \uc2 "It is a step in the right direction, but a plan alone is not going to make much of a difference," said Fauzi Ichsan, Standard Chartered Bank's Jakarta-based economist.  \par \par \uc2 Targeting interest rates was necessary, since higher rates would support the rupiah, but not sufficient, Ichsan said.  \par \par \uc2 "Because, as the rupiah keeps tanking, you need stronger and stronger measures. It has to be introduced in conjunction with other policy measures to stem capital outflow and increase capital inflow."  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian economy is growing comfortably at an annual rate of about 6 percent, but the rupiah <IDR=> has fallen 6 percent this year, adding to the problems of imported inflation at a time of record high oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday it was at 9,830 per dollar, just above a three-year low of 9,900 hit earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia is formally independent. Indonesian law states its mission is "to achieve and maintain rupiah stability", so it could give priority to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and become lax on inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Sanjeev Sanyal, an economist with Deutsche Bank, said Bank Indonesia knew inflation targeting in Indonesia involved a lot of exchange rate management and that it was raising interest rates to support the rupiah.  \par \par \uc2 But according to Ramkishen Rajan, a professor at Singapore's LKY School of Public Policy, such multiple objectives can frustrate central banks' efforts to communicate monetary policy in a straightforward manner.  \par \par \uc2 CREDIBILITY  \par \par \uc2 Some 41 countries target inflation, using interest rates and other tools to keep price rises to an advertised level.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia adopted the policy after agreeing to an International Monetary Fund programme during the 1997/98 Asian financial crisis. Until this month, policy was conducted by steering base money -- cash in circulation in the economy plus bank reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia managed to get annual consumer price inflation down from double-digit rates in 2002 but it remains volatile.  \par \par \uc2 It hit a two-year high of 8.8 percent in March, after the government cut fuel subsidies to ease pressure on the state budget, but had fallen to 7.42 percent by June.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on one-month \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  certificates (SBIs) <ID1MSBI=BIIA>, which is the new policy benchmark, has risen to 8.44 percent from 7.4 percent at the start of the year but is still five points below levels at the end of 2002. On Tuesday, Bank Indonesia announced a target for consumer price inflation of 7 percent by the end of 2005 and a one-month bond yield of 8.5 percent for the next three months.  \par \par \uc2 One big problem for many Asian central banks is political interference, since exchange rate policy is often the domain of the political leadership.  \par \par \uc2 For instance, the Bank of Korea has to balance its policy around the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the won competitive. In \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ichsan said, lawmakers do not want rates to rise too fast and they have been slow in clearing the red tape and rigid labour laws that put off foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 In the end, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to build up credibility, said Huw McKay, regional economist with Westpac Bank in Sydney.  \par \par \uc2 "You can announce a target. But if your market does not respect your ability to achieve that target, expectations will be unchanged," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically, you have to deliver on an inflation target for a sustained period of time, up to a decade, before the market begins implicitly trusting you. And the Indonesian economy hasn't performed in a fashion that has enabled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain credibility."  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-INDONESIA-POLICY (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|IF|SI  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050707e1770007h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
