\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Rupiah Ends Up On  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Support Measures  \b0\par\par\uc2 316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2005\par 13:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones Newswires)--The Indonesian rupiah ended higher Wednesday after hitting a more than three-year low Tuesday, as market sentiment rebounded on new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at IDR9,790, down from IDR9,870 Tuesday. Wednesday's result marked the first time in seven sessions that the rupiah gained against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said a government plan unveiled late Tuesday for Bank Indonesia to directly supply U.S. dollars to the state-owned petroleum firm Pertamina (PTM.YY) brought the dollar down to its opening level of IDR9,830 and limited its peak to IDR9,880 during Wednesday trading.  \par \par \uc2 The plan is aimed at easing the impact on the rupiah of Pertamina's massive appetite for dollars to fund oil imports.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts have attributed the rupiah's recent decline to Pertamina's dollar purchases as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthens globally and oil prices hit record highs. The benchmark August price for crude oil rose 31 U.S. cents to $59.90 per barrel Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The off-market dollar supply mechanism for Pertamina will reduce total dollar market volume by almost 25%, Standard Chartered economist Fauzi Ichsan told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers cautioned that the rupiah remained vulnerable to long-term dollar demand by local companies, including Pertamina.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan was effective...but at the end of the day, corporates still need to buy dollars," a dealer at a foreign bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar will likely trade in a range of IDR9,700 to IDR9,900 Thursday, dealers said. Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with dollar sales through state-owned banks to ensure the rupiah doesn't fall below IDR9,900.  \par \par \uc2 -By Phelim Kyne; Dow Jones Newswires' Jakarta Bureau; 62 21 3983 1277; phelim.kyne@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 06-07-05 1110GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050706e176000if\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
